NON_REGRESSION_BASELINE
- SignalStore hot path must remain local L1 for telemetry ingestion, FSM/reconciliation, session tracking, and snapshot merge context.
- Redis optionality must remain: Redis HSET and Pub/Sub errors cannot block telemetry ingestion or erase local SignalStore state.
- Redis compatibility must preserve HSET key vehicle:{vehicleID}:signals, Pub/Sub channel vehicle_signals, and backlog key signal_log:backlog unless a later prompt adds an explicit migration and compatibility shim.
- Historical reads must continue using signal_log/TimescaleDB and existing signal history paths for charts, point-in-time reconstruction, telemetry traces, and completion logic. Redis must not become the source of history.
- API/frontend contract must remain compatible: route paths, response shapes, and hook URL conventions are not changed in this prompt.
- Docs must describe layered state truthfully: SignalStore L1 + Redis live cache/PubSub/backlog + signal_log durable history. Do not claim Redis replaced SignalStore while SignalStore remains required by the hot path.

SCALE_RISKS
- Redis HSET has no per-signal timestamps. Cross-pod readers using Redis cannot enforce the same 2-minute freshness contract used by FSM reconciliation in internal\fsm\reconciler.go:43-45.
- /signals/{vehicleID}/live currently reads local SignalStore only in internal\api\signal_handler.go:315; a pod without the telemetry stream may return empty or stale data.
- Vehicle state responses currently use local SignalStore through internal\api\vehicle_handler.go:183-190; cross-pod HTTP serving can diverge from the pod receiving MQTT.
- Battery cells and alert test template rendering currently prefer local SignalStore in internal\api\battery_cells_handler.go:181 and internal\api\alert_handler.go:276-277; these are user-facing cross-pod reads that may see pod-local state.
- Redis Pub/Sub SSE is non-durable. It fans out live events but cannot replay missed updates; API reads must be the recovery path for clients.
- SignalHistoryWriter overflow uses Redis backlog as a secondary WAL, but pushToRedisBacklog is explicitly best-effort and logs failures. This must not be confused with live state.
- FSM/reconciliation reads local SignalStore timestamps and should not become active-active across pods without vehicle-owner routing, leases, or pod affinity.
- Several handlers already use Redis for cross-pod reads but fall back to signal_log SnapshotAt(now). Without timestamped Redis values, the fallback does not run when Redis returns old-but-present values.

=== REASONING ===
TARGET_ARCHITECTURE:
- Keep signal.Store as L1 for local hot paths.
- Use Redis-backed live state for cross-pod reads and restart recovery.
- Do not remove signal.Store in Phase 35.
- Do not make Redis a synchronous blocker for telemetry ingestion unless a bounded fallback is present.
REASONING_SUMMARY:
- The current code already has the intended layered shape but not a complete cross-pod read contract. Telemetry writes local L1 first, mirrors Redis asynchronously, and writes durable history asynchronously/buffered.
- Local-hot-path reads are latency-sensitive and timestamp-sensitive. They include telemetry snapshot merge, FSM reconciliation, and session completion/accumulation logic; these should continue to use SignalStore unless a later design provides equivalent local freshness and performance.
- User-facing/API reads are the main scale-out risk. Some already use Redis with signal_log fallback, while /signals/live, vehicle state, battery cell current values, and alert test template rendering still read local SignalStore.
- Redis HSET currently stores decoded values only. Later prompts need a freshness contract before Redis can safely become the default cross-pod live source for all user-facing reads.
- Durable history remains signal_log/TimescaleDB. Redis Pub/Sub and Redis HSET are live/current-state mechanisms, not historical sources.

